<tr><td>Receipts for emergency repairs</td><td>Supports reimbursement for tarping and mitigation.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Common Claim Issues and Insurer Arguments</h2>
<p>Hail claims are often disputed on the details. Arguments we commonly see include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Wear and tear, not hail.&rdquo;</strong> The insurer attributes granule loss or cracking to age, blistering, or manufacturing defects.</li>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Prior storm.&rdquo;</strong> The carrier says damage happened before the policy period or during an earlier, unreported storm. McAllen often sees multiple hail days in a single season, which makes dating the damage important.</li>
<li><strong>&ldquo;Cosmetic only.&rdquo;</strong> Dents to metal roofing or soft metals are labeled cosmetic under an exclusion endorsement.</li>
<li><strong>Repair versus replace.</strong> The estimate allows spot repairs even when discontinued or brittle materials cannot be matched or repaired without further damage.</li>
<li><strong>Missing line items.</strong> Drip edge, starter course, ridge, flashing, and code-required underlayment are left off. Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> looks for these gaps.</li>
</ul>

