<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<p>Several policy features shape what a hail claim actually pays:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductibles.</strong> Many Texas policies carry a separate deductible, often a percentage of the dwelling limit. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV.</strong> Under replacement cost coverage, insurers typically pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are completed. Some roof coverage is ACV-only or follows a payment schedule. Read more in <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> The City of McAllen adopted the 2024 ICC codes effective January 1, 2026. Code-required upgrades during a re-roof are only paid if your policy includes ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Limits.</strong> Other structures and personal property have their own limits.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can estimate how deductibles and depreciation affect a payment with our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a>.</p>

<h2>Hail Damage Beyond McAllen City Limits</h2>
<p>Hail swaths in Hidalgo County rarely stop at a city line. The April 2023 storm hit Peñitas, La Joya, and Mission especially hard, while the 2012 storms crossed McAllen, Mission, Pharr, and Edinburg. If your property is anywhere in the Rio Grande Valley (RGV), the same documentation steps apply. See our <a href="/service-areas/">service areas</a> for the communities we serve.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A licensed Texas public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurance company. On a hail claim, that may include inspecting the property, reviewing the policy, preparing a detailed estimate, gathering weather data, and communicating with the insurer&rsquo;s adjuster. If the claim has already been paid low, we can review it for an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> or discuss whether <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> fits a dispute over the amount of loss. We cannot guarantee a result; outcomes depend on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Give prompt notice</strong> of the loss to your insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Mitigate further damage</strong> &ndash; tarp roof openings and board broken windows, and keep receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Preserve evidence</strong> &ndash; photograph damage and keep samples of damaged materials when practical.</li>
<li><strong>Submit a proof of loss</strong> if requested, within the time the policy requires.</li>
<li><strong>Cooperate</strong> with inspections and reasonable requests for documents.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations and Exclusions</h2>
<p>Common limitations on hail claims include cosmetic damage exclusions (frequently on metal roofs), roof surface payment schedules, ACV-only roof endorsements, exclusions for wear and tear or deterioration, and contractual deadlines for completing repairs to recover depreciation. Many policies also include a deadline to file suit. If you have legal questions about deadlines or a dispute, consult a Texas attorney. If a hail claim has been denied, ask the insurer to explain the denial in writing and identify the policy language it relied on.</p>
